{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788284362-hymiX5kZDjS5e7Uvy584","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let's analyze D1: is this genuinely phase-aware?\n\nThe implementation:\n- `DEV_STARTUP_BUDGET_MS = 180_000` — budget for startup/preflight phase: waiting for the Vite \"Local:\" marker in child stdout/stderr, or child exit, or timeout at 180 s.\n- `FRESH_HTTP_READINESS_BUDGET_MS = 60_000` — budget for HTTP polling, starting **only after** the startup signal resolves (Vite \"Local:\" marker seen).\n\nSo yes, this is genuinely phase-aware: the HTTP readiness budget starts counting only after Vite prints \"Local:\", i.e., after preflight completes. Slow dependency verification consumes the startup budget (180 s), not the HTTP readiness budget (60 s). And the startup signal comes from output scanning (`VITE_READY_MARKER = 'Local:'` in the last 4 KiB of output).\n\nWait — one nuance: the HTTP readiness phase begins when Vite prints \"Local:\". But Vite printing \"Local:\" doesn't necessarily mean the server is listening... Actually it does roughly. Before the fix, what was the old behavior? Let me check the pre-fix version of the probe (parent of 04420a074, i.e., cd2337d36's version of the file — the file was untouched in cd2337d36, so I need origin/main's version or `git show cd2337d36:packages/.../probe-project-boundary-dev.ts`).\n\nLet me read the pre-fix version to compare — that's also needed for D4 (reconstruct pre-fix probe behavior and confirm committed tests fail against it).\n\nAlso there's a subtlety in the startup phase: `startupSignal` resolves when \"Local:\" appears in output. But what if the \"Local:\" marker appeared BEFORE `waitForFreshDevServer` is called? In `runProbe`, `mirrorOutput` starts pumping immediately after spawn, and `waitForFreshDevServer` is called right after — microtask ordering means output events are unlikely to resolve before the race is set up, but even if the signal resolves first, `startupSignal` is a promise, so racing it is fine.\n\nAnother nuance: in the startup loop, if `startupOutcome.kind === 'retry'`, the loop continues. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `options.childStatus.then(...)` is raced inside the loop, but each loop iteration creates a new `.then` on the same promise — fine.\n\nPotential flaw for D2: if the child exits, `childStatus` resolves with a status — the race immediately throws with the real exit code. Prompt? Yes: no sleep needed since the race includes childStatus. Good.\n\nBut — subtle bug risk: in the startup loop, the race includes `options.sleep(...)`. If child exits during a sleep iteration... no, race resolves as soon as childStatus resolves. `Promise.race` resolves with first settled. Fine.\n\nIn the HTTP phase: after a `fetch-error` or non-ok response, the retry race includes childStatus. Good.\n\nWhat if the child exits AND the response succeeds simultaneously? Race picks whichever settled first; if response settles first and is ok, returns response. Edge case fine.\n\nNow: is there a scenario where the startup phase hangs? `startupSignal` resolves on \"Local:\" marker. If Vite prints \"Local:\" to stdout before the deadline, fine. If Vite never starts, timeout at 180 s with message `Generated dev startup/preflight timed out after 180000ms; status=running`. Distinct from the HTTP-failure message `Fresh dev server failed under hostile parent tsconfig after startup: fetch failed; status=running`. D3 satisfied by construction; must verify by execution.\n\nOne more check: `remainingMs` in the startup loop — `Math.min(HTTP_POLL_INTERVAL_MS, remainingMs)`. If remainingMs <= 0 the while condition already failed. Fine.\n\nAnother: after startup completes via marker, HTTP readiness budget starts. But suppose Vite prints \"Local:\" and then the server takes >60 s to accept connections (unlikely since Local: means listening). Fine.\n\nNow, is there a flaw where the HTTP phase's `fetchRoot()` might hang forever (fetch with no timeout)? `Promise.race` with childStatus and... if fetch hangs and child keeps running, the race never resolves; the deadline check happens only after the race settles. Hmm: `while (options.now() < deadline)` — if `fetchRoot()` never settles, the loop body's `await Promise.race` hangs forever, so the 60 s HTTP budget would not actually be enforced. That's a pre-existing behavior pattern though. In practice fetch to a local port fails fast (connection refused). But a hung fetch (e.g. connection accepted but no response) would stall past the deadline. Is that a blocking finding? The issue's acceptance is about slow preflight not consuming HTTP budget, and the poll loop with local fetch errors is fast. The pre-existing code likely had the same shape. Let me check the old version to see if this is new. Also the retry race includes sleep so that one is bounded. The first attempt's fetch is the only unbounded part. In Deno, `fetch` to 127.0.0.1 with no server listening fails almost immediately (ECONNREFUSED). A hung fetch would require the server to accept and never respond — possible if Vite is stuck mid-handshake but it did print Local:. This is an edge; worth noting as non-blocking observation.\n\nLet me now read the pre-fix probe (from cd2337d36) and the test file.","signature":""}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null,"speed":"standard"},"provider":"Z.AI","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"578bc6cf-ec9d-4aea-ae50-b5b4f29f7a32","uuid":"869d7086-b3f1-404a-bf1a-9f49a1bb4c77","timestamp":"2026-09-01T17:39:55.462Z","request_id":"gen-1788284362-hymiX5kZDjS5e7Uvy584"}
