{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788285123-aMyrrdl7lI4Q40PjhNSF","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent — decisive: against a \"single raised constant\" implementation, the committed suite fails test 1 exactly as it should (`Fresh dev server failed: server did not answer; status=running` — the simulated 179999 ms preflight ate the shared deadline). So the suite is NOT vacuous: it discriminates phase-awareness from a raised constant.\n\nInteresting: the failure message was \"server did not answer\" not \"fetch failed\" — because with now=179999 the first while check passed (179999 < 180000), then fetch was called... wait, expected fetch-error 'fetch failed'. Hmm, the message says 'server did not answer' meaning the HTTP loop never ran a fetch. Let me think: naive: deadline = 0 + 180000 = 180000. Test sets now = 179999 (before resolving startup). Startup loop: race → startup resolves → 'started'. HTTP loop: `while (options.now() < deadline)` → 179999 < 180000 → true → race fetchRoot() → fetch #1 rejects → fetch-error → lastError='fetch failed'; remaining = 180000-179999 = 1 > 0 → `await options.sleep(min(250,1)=1)` → test's fake sleep: `now += 1` → 180000. Loop check: 180000 < 180000 false → exit → throw with lastError='fetch failed'.\n\nBut observed message is 'server did not answer' — meaning the loop exited WITHOUT a fetch attempt... Ah wait — test 1's fake sleep for the naive implementation during the STARTUP phase: sleep(250) advances now by 250 each iteration. When does the test's `now = DEV_STARTUP_BUDGET_MS - 1` assignment happen relative to the loop iterations? In test 1, after `await Promise.resolve()`, it sets now=179999 and resolves startup. With the naive implementation, the loop is the same shape. Hmm, but why didn't fetch get called?\n\nWait — maybe the startup loop exited via deadline before the test set now! Sequence: probe called → first race: startupSignal pending, childStatus pending, sleep(250) → resolves 'retry' after the test's single microtask? Order: test calls probe() (runs to first await), test awaits Promise.resolve() (queues T1), M1 (sleep.then → retry) and M2 (race resolution) run first, probe loops, now=250, new race, M3/M4 queued... then T1 runs: now=179999, startup.resolve(). Probe continues: race sees startup resolved → 'started' → startupComplete=true, break. Same as before.\n\nHmm wait, actually — maybe the difference: in the naive implementation I wrote, the race order puts `startupSignal.then` FIRST. Same as original. So why 'server did not answer'?\n\nUnless... the test's second `await` interleaving differs: after startup resolves, the probe breaks out of the loop, then the HTTP while check: now()=179999 < 180000 → race(fetchRoot()..., childStatus...). fetchRoot: fetchCalls += 1 → 1, returns Promise.reject(TypeError('fetch failed')) → 'fetch-error' → lastError = 'fetch failed'. remainingMs = deadline - now() = 180000 - 179999 = 1. `if (remainingMs <= 0) break` — 1 > 0, so sleep(1) → now=180000. Loop: exit → throw 'Fresh dev server failed: fetch failed; status=running'.\n\nObserved: 'server did not answer'. So the HTTP loop body never ran → the while condition was false at entry → now() >= deadline at HTTP-loop entry. That means now >= 180000 when the startup phase ended. So the startup loop must have exited via `!startupComplete`... no, that throws the timeout error. So the loop exited with startupComplete=true AND now >= 180000. How? The test set now=179999... unless the test's assignment ran when now was ALREADY ≥ 180000-? No — the test sets now = 179999 exactly, overwriting whatever accumulated. So now=179999, then startup.resolve(). Then probe's pending race from BEFORE... the probe was in a race with sleep(250) — the fake sleep resolves and advances now by 250 → now = 179999+250 = 180249! THAT's it. The test's fake sleep advances `now` when called, and there was an in-flight sleep initiated before startup resolved. Sequence: probe in race with sleep(250) (already advanced now by 250 when called? No — the fake sleep advances now when INVOKED: `sleep: (ms) => { if (ms === BUDGET) pending; now += ms; return resolve; }` — it advances now synchronously at call time, not resolution time!\n\nSo in test 1: at probe start, sleep(250) called → now becomes 250 immediately (synchronously, before the first await). Then the test's `await Promise.resolve()` → T1 sets now = 179999 (overwrites 250). startup.resolve(). Probe's race → 'started'. Startup loop breaks. now=179999 < 180000 → HTTP loop entered... that's what I predicted. Hmm.\n\nBut with the NAIVE implementation, the race construction order differs slightly: `options.startupSignal.then(...)`, `options.childStatus.then(...)`, `options.sleep(250).then(...)` — same. After 'started', break. Then HTTP while: now() = 179999 < 180000 → enters. fetch #1 → 'fetch failed'...\n\nObserved 'server did not answer' though. Let me reconsider: maybe the startup loop ran MORE iterations before T1, and at the moment the test set now=179999, there was ALSO an in-flight race where the sleep was already invoked → but sleep advances now at invoke time... Each loop iteration calls sleep exactly once (advancing now by 250 synchronously). The loop only continues via microtasks. T1 was queued after M1/M2, so only one sleep happened before T1 → now=250 → overwritten to 179999.\n\nWait — unless the naive startup race's `sleep` gets invoked a second time between T1 and the startup resolution! After T1 sets now=179999 and startup.resolve(), the probe's pending race from the second iteration... The second iteration's race: startupSignal.then (queued when?), childStatus.then, sleep(250) → invoked NOW → now = 179999+250 = 180249! Then race settles: startupSignal already resolved → but race settles with whichever settles FIRST — childStatus.pending, startup resolved at T1 → startupSignal.then handler was queued at race construction (microtask), sleep resolved → 'retry' also queued. Order within the race: handlers attached in order [startup, childStatus, sleep]. startupSignal.then callback queued at construction; sleep's .then callback queued after it. Microtask order: startup-'started' handler runs first → race settles 'started'. But the sleep(250) was still INVOKED (side effect: now += 250 → 180249) before the race settled. Then 'started' → break. HTTP while: now()=180249 >= 180000 → skip → throw 'server did not answer'.\n\nSo the naive failure is caused by one extra 250 ms sleep advancing the shared clock past the deadline — even MORE clearly demonstrating the shared-deadline defect (a single in-flight poll tick consumes the readiness window). Either way: the committed test 1 FAILS against the single-constant implementation, and passes against the phase-aware one. The discriminating power stands.\n\nNow the end-to-end probe runs against the real fixture. Sequential, each timed:\n\n1. RED (pre-fix) probe, PREFLIGHT_DELAY_MS=65000, DEV_MODE=serve → expect fail ~60 s \"Fresh dev server failed ... fetch failed\".\n2. HEAD probe, PREFLIGHT_DELAY_MS=65000, serve → expect success ~65 s \"Fresh dev server answered 200\".\n3. HEAD probe, DEV_MODE=exit → expect fail fast \"Generated dev process exited during startup/preflight; status=5\".\n4. HEAD probe, DEV_MODE=noserve (prints Local:, never serves) → expect fail ~60 s \"Fresh dev server failed ... after startup: fetch failed; status=running\".\n\nProbe invocation: `deno run --allow-all <probe.ts> <projectRoot> <appName>`; appName = 'demo'; projectRoot = fixture dir. The probe spawns `deno task --cwd <root>/apps/demo dev --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5199 --strictPort` with cwd=projectRoot. `deno task` needs deno.json in appRoot — present. Extra args appended to the task command line: `deno run --allow-net dev-script.ts --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5199 --strictPort` — deno run passes them to dev-script.ts as Deno.args (ignored). Good.\n\nNote: pre-fix probe is a top-level script — running it